      'SEBoK’s definitions of a model all treat it as a representation of selected aspects of a domain, chosen for a set of concerns and related to the system by a mapping; many models may be needed for one system of interest (R33a p.290). Abstract models rely on a modelling language with an abstract syntax (constructs and rules), a concrete syntax (symbols or text) and semantics (meaning), and a modelling tool typically checks whether a model conforms to the language’s rules (R33a p.291–292). That is exactly what the course-designated checker does, and nothing more.',
      'In the INCOSE definition SEBoK quotes, MBSE is the formalized application of modelling to support requirements, design, analysis, verification and validation from the conceptual phase onward, with models as the primary, controlled artefacts where a document-centric approach manages documents (R33a p.292). The main system model can serve as a single source of truth in a standardised language such as SysML (R28 p.370); formality and abstraction matter most among model properties, and an effective model complies strictly with its language and describes at least the functional and structural architecture (R28 p.370–371).',
      'SEBoK credits MBSE with surfacing inconsistencies while the model is built, reducing miscommunication through one source, and letting verification and validation start earlier; digital models can also propagate change and generate documents — “if implemented appropriately” (R28 p.373). The benefits shrink when the model falls out of date (R28 p.373), and where stakeholders expect documents, these are generated from the model as static representations at milestones (R28 p.374).',
