    id: 'w9-language',
    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'The SysML v2 language: architecture, packages, names, and definition versus usage',
    paragraphs: [
      'Language architecture. SysML v2 is built in four layers (R70 sl.3–4; R90 sl.8). Root holds syntactic elements without model-level semantics (element, namespace, relationship, import). Core adds type, classifier, feature and multiplicity and relationships such as specialization, with a direct mapping to formal logic. Kernel completes the Kernel Modeling Language (KerML) with classes, data types, behaviours and connectors. Systems is SysML proper — definitions and usages of attributes, parts, ports, actions, requirements, views and more — with libraries that include ISQ, SI and ScalarValues (R90 sl.17). Most SysML elements specialize KerML elements (S06 p.47), which is why specialization, subsetting and redefinition are defined in KerML.',
      'Packages and imports. A package is a namespace and a container for its owned members; an import adds one member or all members of another package; a name with spaces is quoted; a qualified name joins names with “::” (R70 sl.5; R90 sl.17, sl.21). Members are public by default; a public import re-exports what it imports, a private one does not. In the 2026-08 notation every import’s visibility is written explicitly, and private is advised unless re-export is required (R70 sl.6), so the course model starts with private import ISQ::*; private import SI::*; private import ScalarValues::*;.',
      'Names, short names and IDs. The tool’s element ID cannot be written in the notation; instead an element has a name and a short name, the short name between < and > before the name (S06 p.49; R90 sl.22), described as “a user or tool selected id” (R90 sl.22). The course uses it for its IDs (lab plan C.2 rule 3). A basic name starts with a letter or underscore, continues with letters, digits or underscores, and is not a reserved word; any other name is an unrestricted name in single quotes (S06 p.49–50). PHY-003 has hyphens, so its short name is <\'PHY-003\'>, and every reference quotes it too: Physical::aeroLink.\'PHY-003\'.',
      'Definition and usage. A definition classifies a kind of element; a usage uses a definition in a context and is always defined by at least one definition of its kind, or implicitly by the most general one (S06 p.63). One definition can be used in many contexts (R90 sl.12). Usages nested in a definition are its features: a feature relates each instance of its featuring definition to values of its own definition, as a mass feature relates each vehicle to its mass (S06 p.63); a usage directly in a package is a generic feature that can be specialized in context (S06 p.64). So part aircraft : EVTOLAircraft[12] says that any AeroLink system has twelve aircraft values; it creates no aircraft. A part definition defines things that exist in space and time; an attribute definition defines data, may not have composite features, and its usages are referential (R70 sl.9; S06 p.77).',
      'SysML v2 has no system, subsystem, assembly or component constructs: an entity with structure and behaviour is a part (S06 p.48). SysML v1’s blocks and value properties become part definitions and attribute usages; whether the checker tolerates the v1 words is one of your predictions two blocks below.',
    ],
