    id: 'w9-relationships',
    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'Specialization, subsetting and redefinition — three relationships that look alike',
    paragraphs: [
      'Specialization (subclassification). A specialized definition classifies a subset of what its general definition classifies, inherits its features and can add its own; a definition can specialize several; :> means specializes (R70 sl.10; S06 p.64, p.72). An abstract definition’s instances must also be instances of a concrete specialization (R70 sl.10; S06 p.72). In KerML terms, the extent of the specific type is a subset of the extent of the general one (R87 p.53).',
      'Subsetting. Subsetting asserts that, in any common context, the values of one feature are a subset of the values of another, written subsets or :> (R70 sl.11). The subsetting usage may constrain definition and multiplicity — S06’s truck subsets its wheels with four named wheels — and it is an additional feature: the subsetted one is still there (S06 p.64).',
      'Redefinition. Redefinition is a kind of subsetting in which the redefining usage replaces the redefined one in its context (S06 p.65). It can change the name, specialize the type or constrain the multiplicity of an inherited feature; :>> means redefines, and part redefines cyl[4] keeps the name (R70 sl.12). Usages can redefine inherited features “to any level of nesting” (R70 sl.15) and can be specialized by other usages, as smallVehicle :> vehicle (R70 sl.16). S06’s truck redefines its fuel as DieselFuel, so its fuel must be diesel (S06 p.65); S10 binds a value to an inherited attribute by redefining it (R70 sl.13).',
      'One symbol, two meanings. After a definition’s name, :> is specialization; after a usage’s name, it is subsetting, so what follows must be a feature. A usage’s three owned specializations are feature typing (:), subsetting (:>) and redefinition (:>>) (S06 p.72). An attribute, item, part or port usage owned by a definition or usage, with no explicit subsetting or redefinition, defaults to exactly one value; a package-level usage does not (S06 p.73). A redefinition restricts what it inherits — an engine with four to six cylinders redefined to four, or six (R70 sl.12); what goes wrong when one does not is for the graduate reading (R87 p.61–62) and the advanced block.',
    ],
