      'Optional graduate memo, model answer: (a) HubVertiport’s extent is a subset of Vertiport’s; its features are necessary conditions (two high-power turnaround chargers), not sufficient ones (R87 p.52–53); hubs is a feature whose values, on each network, are among its sites and are HubVertiports; alt004Network’s hubs has exactly four values on each value of alt004Network. (b) turnaroundChargers values are also chargers values on each hub (R87 p.61); at a spoke the redefining chargers has the same values as the inherited one, so every charger there is a StandardCharger (R87 p.62). (c) pc4 breaks the rule that a redefining multiplicity allow only cardinalities consistent with the redefined one (widening 3 to 4, R87 p.62), and is also unsatisfiable because its values must equal those of a 3-valued and a 4-valued feature; the tool reports only “should not have larger multiplicity upper bound”, the same warning it gives a satisfiable widening; fix with siblings, as the listing does. (d) Lines 15 and 18 bind ratings 350 kW and 150 kW, so no Charger is both kinds; line 29 makes every charger at a spoke a StandardCharger; line 26 puts two HighPowerChargers among every hub’s chargers; so no HubVertiport is a SpokeVertiport and hubs and spokes share no value in any network; with the counts, they cover the sites in both configurations, but not in the definition. Deleting either binding removes the entailment; the checker accepts both versions. The asserted constraints state the property; the checker resolves their names but does not evaluate them (a deliberately false constraint also passes). Validated model answer: sysml/instructor/week09/memo_d_model_answer.sysml (instructor only, not bundled). Credit runs reported honestly.',
